Ladybird gives a table two boxes: a wrapper that takes the table’s
position, float, margins and z-index, and a table box inside it with
those reset. The table box now gives them up whenever the box tree
builds it, inside a new wrapper or one it kept.

Gecko splits a table the same way, into an nsTableWrapperFrame and an
nsTableFrame. nsCSSFrameConstructor::ConstructTable() always builds the
two together, giving the wrapper the ::-moz-table-wrapper style, which
inherits position, float, margins and z-index from the table. The inner
frame keeps those values, and its code ignores them.

Blink and WebKit build a single box for a table (a LayoutTable or a
RenderTable), which takes the table’s position itself. So they have no
wrapper to keep in step with the table’s box.
